title: SecondLoopAaldenburg1
tags:
---

// plays when first talking to Aaldenburg, when getting the radio quest


<< if $PsychMajor == true >>
    PSY: Her mannerisms are hard to parse without getting dizzy. 
    PSY: Your psychology know-how might not get you very far with her, though you can definitely tell she must have some sort of neurodevelopmental disorder.
<< elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
    ART: The message she broadcasts is clear: She’s a serious woman of numbers and facts, and she has no care for whimsical nonsense. 
    ART: It’s an ironic contrast with the way she hops between stations-- almost like she's dancing. 
    ART: Her half-black, half-red lipstick is quite pretty too.
<< elseif $TechMajor == true >>
	TECH: Before you’ve even talked to her, you already feel a rare kinship with her. 
    TECH: This is someone who sees the world exactly as it is, just like you: A huge complex machine, full of levers to pull, gears to oil, shiny lights to read and reactions to measure. 
<< endif >>

<< if $ThisLoopRadioTrigger == false >>
	AALDENBURG: (Does not notice/ignores SASHA)
    << jump SecondLoopAaldenburgQuit >>
<< endif >>
<< set $ThisLoopMetAaldenburg = true >>

//If the player knows her name from Loop 1
-> "Excuse me, Dr. Aaldenburg?" << if DoesHeKnow("AaldenburgTarot") == true >>
	SASHA:  "Excuse me, Dr. Aaldenburg?" 
    DR. AALDENBURG: ([i]very[/i] slowly comes to a halt)
	DR. AALDENBURG: What is the matter?
	DR. AALDENBURG: (finally looks at SASHA, surprised) I have never seen you before. Are you from outside Solster?
-> "Excuse me, Miss?"
	SASHA: "Excuse me, Miss?"
    DR. AALDENBURG: (stops abruptly, still holding a pen in one hand and a pipette in the other)
	DR. AALDENBURG: Doctor. Not miss.
    DR. AALDENBURG: (finally looks at SASHA, surprised) I have never seen you before. Are you from outside Solster?
-> "Could you stop for a second?"
	SASHA: "Could you stop for a second?"
	DR. AALDENBURG: (ignores for a few seconds, before slowly halting)
    DR. AALDENBURG What is the matter?
    You can finally take a good look at her. An id-card on her lapel identifies her as "Dr. Aaldenburg - Naturalist Physicist - Nauka Square".
    DR. AALDENBURG: (finally looks at SASHA, surprised) I have never seen you before. Are you from outside Solster?

<<Learn AaldenburgTarot>>
-> "I’m Sasha. I was born here, but I went to college away from here."
	SASHA: "I’m Sasha. I was born here, but I went to college away from here."
    DR. AALDENBURG: I suspected so. Your accent says Solster, your outfit says student. Graduate now, I take it?
	DR. AALDENBURG: Graduates of higher education are an uncommon, but welcome sight here in Solster. What did you major in?
-> "No, I’m from here. My name is Sasha."
	SASHA:  "No, I’m from here. My name is Sasha."
    DR. AALDENBURG: I can tell from your accent, yes. Yet I have not seen you or heard from you before, and you seem hard to miss in a town this size.
    DR. AALDENBURG: Are you a college student by chance?
	-> "I was. I graduated."
		SASHA: "I was. I graduated."
		DR. AALDENBURG: (obviously pleased with herself) The hypothesis is proved correct. You do still have that rebellious college student aesthetic. It must have been a recent graduation. 
        << if $ArtMajor == true >>
		    ART: Curious that she is familiar with the ‘college student’ aesthetic. It makes you wonder what she was like back in her own college days. You have a feeling she was quite different to how she is now…
        << endif >>
		DR. AALDENBURG: Graduates of higher education are an uncommon, but welcome sight here in Solster. What did you major in?
	-> "How did you guess that?"
		SASHA: "How did you guess that?"
		DR. AALDENBURG: (amused) I did not guess it, I hypothesised it based on the information I could gather.
		DR. AALDENBURG: You look college aged, and your outfit has some of that rebellious aesthetic that students love. It would explain why I had never seen you before, too.
        << if $ArtMajor == true >>
		    ART: Curious that she is familiar with the ‘college student’ aesthetic. It makes you wonder what she was like back in her own college days. You have a feeling she was quite different to how she is now…
        << endif >>
		DR. AALDENBURG: Since you are back at Solster, I can further hypothesise that you are either visiting family, or graduated. If I were to make an actual ‘guess’, I would say graduated.
		DR. AALDENBURG: This town is not a place one comes back to for the holidays. I could not imagine all that travel to come here, for [i]what[/i]?
		DR. AALDENBURG: Graduates of higher education are an uncommon, but welcome sight here in Solster. What did you major in?
-> "I’m Sasha. I’m pretty much an outsider here."
	SASHA: "I’m Sasha. I’m pretty much an outsider here."
    DR. AALDENBURG: Pretty [i]much[/i]? That is curiously non-committal language to use to describe your status. 
	DR. AALDENBURG: (looks SASHA up and down curiously) Your accent sounds quite Solsterian for an outsider too. Are you a college student by chance?
        -> "I was. I graduated."
			SASHA: "I was. I graduated."
		    DR. AALDENBURG: (obviously pleased) The hypothesis is proved correct. You do still have that rebellious college student aesthetic. It must have been a recent graduation. 
			<< if $ArtMajor == true >>
				ART: Curious that she is familiar with the ‘college student’ aesthetic. It makes you wonder what she was like back in her own college days. You have a feeling she was quite different to how she is now…
			<< endif >>
		    DR. AALDENBURG: Graduates of higher education are an uncommon, but welcome sight here in Solster. What did you major in?
		-> "How did you guess that?"
			SASHA: "How did you guess that?"
			DR. AALDENBURG: (amused) I did not guess it, I hypothesised it based on the information I could gather.
			DR. AALDENBURG: You look college aged, and your outfit has some of that rebellious aesthetic that students love. It would explain why I had never seen you before, too.
			<< if $ArtMajor == true >>
				ART: Curious that she is familiar with the ‘college student’ aesthetic. It makes you wonder what she was like back in her own college days. You have a feeling she was quite different to how she is now…
			<< endif >>
			DR. AALDENBURG: Since you are back at Solster, I can further hypothesise that you are either visiting family, or graduated. If I were to make an actual ‘guess’, I would say graduated.
			DR. AALDENBURG: This town is not a place one comes back to for the holidays. I could not imagine all that travel to come here, for [i]what[/i]?
			DR. AALDENBURG: Graduates of higher education are an uncommon, but welcome sight here in Solster. What did you major in?

<< if $PsychMajor == true >>
	-> "Psychology."
		SASHA: "Psychology."
	    DR. AALDENBURG: A people-person I see. (tenser) I’m sure Dr. Wisteria will be delighted.
<< elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
	-> "I went to art school."
		SASHA: "I went to art school."
	    DR. AALDENBURG: (brief grimace) Really? I took you for more of an intellectual sort. 
        DR. AALDENBURG: Well, at least you're bringing something new to the town. Artistic pursuits are still more stimulating than just staring at the lake, how most people spend their free time here.
<< elseif $TechMajor == true >>
    -> "Engineering."
		SASHA: "Engineering."
	    DR. AALDENBURG: (slight glee, quickly steeled) It is always pleasant to meet another fellow professional in the field of the practical sciences.
	    DR. AALDENBURG: Perhaps you are still too green to call you  a professional, but I am sure we will get along well.
	    DR. AALDENBURG: My expertise is in Chemistry, but I dabble in various fields. I have little other choice, given the staffing of my laboratory.
<< endif >>
NARRATOR: A nearby machine suddenly emits a beep. Aaldenburg turns her attention to it, turning a dial down and writing a reading from its display on her notebook.
DR. AALDENBURG: I would not mind chatting with you further on another occasion, but I am rather busy at this time, as you can see. Was there a reason you came to see me?

-> "I just traveled back in time a full day after seeing the void destroy Solster."
	SASHA:"I just traveled back in time a full day after seeing the void destroy Solster."
	DR. AALDENBURG: No, you did not. Anything else?
	-> "My radio is acting very strange. I thought maybe someone here at Nauka Square would now what is happening to it"
		SASHA: "My radio is acting very strange. I thought maybe someone here at Nauka Square would now what is happening to it"
		DR. AALDENBURG: (immediately stops writing) You have a radio? They are quite rare here in Solster. 
		DR. AALDENBURG: I am not a repairwoman, but you have made me curious now. Show me.
			-> "Wisteria sent me here to get my radio fixed."
				SASHA: "Wisteria sent me here to get my radio fixed."
				DR. AALDENBURG: (stares at SASHA blankly, almost bitingly)
				DR. AALDENBURG: Oh, of course [i]she[/i] is behind this. She does not miss an opportunity to try to disrupt my work on the day of the parade.
				DR. AALDENBURG: Fine. Show me that radio. 
 -> "My radio is acting very strange. I thought maybe someone here at Nauka Square would now what is happening to it"
	SASHA: "My radio is acting very strange. I thought maybe someone here at Nauka Square would now what is happening to it"
	DR. AALDENBURG: (immediately stops writing) You have a radio? They are quite rare here in Solster. 
	DR. AALDENBURG: I am not a repairwoman, but you have made me curious now. Show me.
-> "Wisteria sent me here to get my radio fixed."
	SASHA: "Wisteria sent me here to get my radio fixed."
	DR. AALDENBURG: (stares at SASHA blankly, almost bitingly)
	DR. AALDENBURG: Oh, of course [i]she[/i] is behind this. She does not miss an opportunity to try to disrupt my work on the day of the parade.
	DR. AALDENBURG: Fine. Show me that radio. 

RADIO: ([i]static[/i])
DR. AALDENBURG: Ah, an Adalard 22. It [i]is[/i] a good model, one of the better domestic radios one can find in the market. You have a good eye.
<< if $TechMajor == true >>
    TECH: She has a good eye for this as well, for someone whose speciality lies not in machines. Judging by all the contraptions in this lab, she must have a good amount of knowledge of electronics as well.
<< endif >>
NARRATOR: Aaldenburg places the radio on her work desk and begins her examination. She looks at it from every angle, touching every button and turning every dial.
NARRATOR: In the meantime, you explain to her what you experienced, the sudden static and interference, and the voice mixed within.
NARRATOR: Eventually, she takes a screwdriver and opens it up, looking carefully at the mechanisms within without touching them.
NARRATOR: After a few minutes, she reassembles your radio and hands it back. Seems she put it back together perfectly, to your relief.

DR. AALDENBURG: There do not appear to be any defects or malfunctions on the radio itself. Thus, there is nothing to [i]fix[/i] here.
DR. AALDENBURG: But… I admit I [i]am[/i] curious about that interference you encountered. 
DR. AALDENBURG: As I said, there are very few radios in Solster outside of this laboratory. You might have stumbled upon a novel phenomenon that has not been recorded before. This warrants further exploration.
DR. AALDENBURG: (suddenly walks off)
...
DR. AALDENBURG: (returns with a small device, puts it in SASHA's hands)
DR. AALDENBURG: You are going to do some field work. Take this GPS, and go into the town to find some places where the radio interference is present. Understood?

-> "How does this ‘GPS’ work? I've never heard of it."
	SASHA: "How does this ‘GPS’ work? I've never heard of it."
    DR. AALDENBURG: It is a [i]fairly][/i] new invention, with limited application. I got one for testing from my old university, as Solster is perfect grounds for a trial.
	DR. AALDENBURG: The GPS sends and receives constant signals to and from a receptor here at Nauka Square. 
    DR. AALDENBURG: By measuring the time between signals and their direction, the device can accurately display your exact coordinates within the town as a vector from the receptor.
    DR. AALDENBURG: It will let you note down your position in numbers, much more precise than using a map.
    -> "What do I have to do exactly?"
		SASHA: "What do I have to do exactly?"
	    DR. AALDENBURG: We are going to triangulate the source of the radio interference, by finding several locations around Solster where your radio experiences said interference.
	    DR. AALDENBURG: We already know the Star Hospital is one such location. If you can find three more places where your radio reacts to the interference, we should be able to locate where it is coming from.
	    DR. AALDENBURG: Be precise. If your radio starts making that static noise, then move around your current area until you locate the exact position where the static is the strongest.
	    DR. AALDENBURG: Do you understand your task now?
-> "What do I have to do exactly?"
	SASHA: "What do I have to do exactly?"
	DR. AALDENBURG: We are going to triangulate the source of the radio interference, by finding several locations around Solster where your radio experiences said interference.
	DR. AALDENBURG: We already know the Star Hospital is one such location. If you can find three more places where your radio reacts to the interference, we should be able to locate where it is coming from.
	DR. AALDENBURG: Be precise. If your radio starts making that static noise, then move around your current area until you locate the exact position where the static is the strongest.
	DR. AALDENBURG: Do you understand your task now?

//Unlocks after "what do I do exactly?"
-> "Any ideas of where I might find this static?"
	SASHA: "Any ideas of where I might find this static?"
	DR. AALDENBURG: (after a long thought) The first time the static occurred while you were at the Hospital, right?
	DR. AALDENBURG: The hospital does have some machines in it that could have played a factor, but we can’t state that for certain.
	DR. AALDENBURG: My advice would be to stay observant of what is around you once you do find a place where the static is present. Perhaps in similar places, the static will be present too.
	DR. AALDENBURG: As to how you should find your first place with static, that is merely a matter of legwork. 
    DR. AALDENBURG: You could try to investigate areas of Solster that you have not gone to yet or have transited less since you came back. That would be a good course of action. 
        -> "You’re not coming with me?"
			SASHA: "You’re not coming with me?"
            DR. AALDENBURG: Did you forget already when, approximately seven minutes ago, I said I was currently busy? Don't students do practical assignments nowadays?
            << if $PsychMajor == true >>
                PSY: You did do some practical assignments, but you suspect her standards for what "practical" means are stricter than those of your teachers.
	            PSY: It did not involve anywhere as much walking as she probably believes is necessary.
            << elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
                ART: You’ve been to places for your study, though mainly museums and parks for live drawing. Not so much waddling in mud or climbing up trees to find a radio signal.
            << elseif $TechMajor == true >>
                TECH: They did not, no. Your practical assignments primarily took place in a workshop, one not too different from Nauka Square.
            << endif >>
 -> "You’re not coming with me?"
	SASHA: "You’re not coming with me?"
	DR. AALDENBURG: Did you forget already when, approximately seven minutes ago, I said I was currently busy? Don't students do practical assignments nowadays?
	<< if $PsychMajor == true >>
		PSY: You did do some practical assignments, but you suspect her standards for what "practical" means are stricter than those of your teachers.
		PSY: It did not involve anywhere as much walking as she probably believes is necessary.
	<< elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
		ART: You’ve been to places for your study, though mainly museums and parks for live drawing. Not so much waddling in mud or climbing up trees to find a radio signal.
	<< elseif $TechMajor == true >>
		TECH: They did not, no. Your practical assignments primarily took place in a workshop, one not too different from Nauka Square.
	<< endif >>
		-> "Any ideas of where I might find this static?"
			SASHA: "Any ideas of where I might find this static?"
			DR. AALDENBURG: (after a long thought) The first time the static occurred while you were at the Hospital, right?
			DR. AALDENBURG: The hospital does have some machines in it that could have played a factor, but we can’t state that for certain.
			DR. AALDENBURG: My advice would be to stay observant of what is around you once you do find a place where the static is present. Perhaps in similar places, the static will be present too.
			DR. AALDENBURG: As to how you should find your first place with static, that is merely a matter of legwork. 
			DR. AALDENBURG: You could try to investigate areas of Solster that you have not gone to yet or have transited less since you came back. That would be a good course of action. 

//Unlocks when you have selected at least the "What do I do exactly" option.
-> "Okay, I think I am ready to go out then."
	SASHA: "Okay, I think I am ready to go out then."
	DR. AALDENBURG: Good. You can come back once you have found three static locations, other than the hospital.
	DR. AALDENBURG: Be careful with the GPS. If you break it, you [i]will[/i] pay for it.
	NARRATOR: You take a look at the device in your hand. The screen reads [2.38161, 5.19372]. 
    << if $TechMajor == true >>
        TECH: The display is likely the x and y factors of a distance vector between the device and one of the antennas you saw on the roof of the building.
	    TECH: Is it connecting to that big, gold antenna you saw? This could be something to ask about later.
	<< endif >>
	NARRATOR: Now, it is time to head out into Solster.
    << Learn $RadioQuestStarted >>
===














title: SecondLoopAaldenburg2
tags:
---

DR. AALDENBURG: Sasha, my new assistant, returns. Have you completed your task?

-> "Not yet."
	SASHA: "Not yet."
    DR. AALDENBURG: Then why did you return? You are [i]not[/i] giving up already.
    << jump SecondLoopAaldenburgQuit >>
-> "What did I need to do again?"
	SASHA: "What did I need to do again?"
    DR. AALDENBURG: Did you not note it down? The most important rule for any researcher to follow is to always note down anything important.  
	DR. AALDENBURG: Did you not find my assignment to be important enough? I do [i]not[/i] like repeating myself, Sasha. (grumbles) I saw you looking down into that notebook of yours..
	DR. AALDENBURG: Just go find three spots on the town where your radio catches the static interference, and note down the location using the GPS. (huffs, and returns to her work)
     << jump SecondLoopAaldenburgQuit >>
-> "I have all three coordinates you wanted." <<if DoesHeKnow("AllThreeRadios")>>
	SASHA: "I have all three coordinates you wanted."
    DR. AALDENBURG: Excellent! You are a reliable assistant, Sasha. 
	DR. AALDENBURG: (approaches hastily, but hesitates to take SASHA's notebook)
	-> Give her your notebook.
            DR. AALDENBURG: (Takes it carefully)
			<<jump RadioQuestCompleteAal>>
	-> Read out the coordinates for her.
            DR. AALDENBURG: Hold on, [i]hold on[/i]. Don’t be so fast.
			DR. AALDENBURG: (takes out her own notebook, jotting down your observations)
			<<jump RadioQuestCompleteAal>>
===










title: RadioQuestCompleteAal
---
<< Learn RadioQuestComplete>>        
DR. AALDENBURG: Interesting… This might confirm my suspicions.
DR. AALDENBURG: (moves to a map of Solster, marking down the coordinates)
DR. AALDENBURG: Did you notice anything peculiar in the areas where you found the static?
    -> "Nothing that springs to mind."
		SASHA: "Nothing that springs to mind."
        DR. AALDENBURG: Unfortunate. Not a setback, however. The data is quite clear here.
		DR. AALDENBURG: (points to the island on the lake)
    -> "There were electric cables near all of them that seemed to lead towards the lake."
		SASHA: "There were electric cables near all of them that seemed to lead towards the lake."
		DR. AALDENBURG: (pleased)Just as I thought! Do you know where all the electricity in Solster comes from, Sasha?
		DR. AALDENBURG: (continues without waiting) The island on the lake is the source of all electricity in this town, and undeniably the source of the interference. And doesn't there happen to be something intru

DR. AALDENBURG: All the coordinates point towards the source [i]being[/i] the island. Being separate from the rest of the town, there must be a radio device there to contact the mainland in case of emergencies.
DR. AALDENBURG: Try a frequency of 99.235 Hertz
-> Tune the radio to 99.235
	SASHA: (tunes radio)
	RADIO: (static, light buzzing)
	RADIO: (suddenly gets louderr)

???: Help me! I am trapped! Can someone hear me?
-> "I can hear you!"
	SASHA: "I can hear you!"
	???: Oh, thank Star! I thought nobody was going to answer.
	RAYNA: I’m Rayna, and I’m trapped in a [i]really[/i] weird situation.
	RAYNA: W-Who are you?
-> "Who are you?"
	SASHA: "Who are you?"
	???: Oh, thank Star! My name is Rayna, and I’m trapped in a [i]really[/i] weird situation.
	RAYNA: Who are you?
-> "Are you trapped in my radio?"
	SASHA: "Are you trapped in my radio?"
	???: N-No, no. Please, this is not time for jokes. I’m trapped somewhere else, but something [i]really[/i] weird is going on.
	RAYNA: My name is Rayna. Who are you?

//After asking what your name is
-> "It’s Sasha. What is going on with you?"
	SASHA: "It’s Sasha. What is going on with you?"
    RAYNA: Okay, Sasha. I don’t know if you can help, but I don’t know who I could even ask.
	RAYNA: This will be hard to believe, but-
	RAYNA: (Audible swallow) I think I might be stuck in a time loop--  I know it sounds absurd! But [i]please[/i] I need you to hear me out.
	-> "It's not weird. I'm right here with you."
		SASHA: "It's not weird. I'm right here with you."
		RAYNA: (silence, followed by a shaky breath of relief)
		RAYNA: By the grace of Star, it looks like I'm not alone anymore.
	-> "Did you see the end of the world too, when the parade ended?"
		SASHA: "Did you see the end of the world too, when the parade ended?"
		RAYNA: I did. And you… you did too?
		RAYNA: You’re in this loop too, aren’t you?



<< jump SecondLoopAaldenburg3 >>










===

title: SecondLoopAaldenburg3
tags:
---
-> "How did you experience the loop?" << if $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Experience == false >>
	SASHA: "How did you experience the loop?" 
    RAYNA: I didn't really see all that much, to be honest. I was just in my room, and suddenly, the lights went out.
	RAYNA: Not suddenly, as in a blackout, but gradually, like the light itself was being devoured.
	RAYNA: I just stood there frozen, cowering. I didn’t know what was happening, and I just saw death approaching me.
	RAYNA: Then when the darkness finally got me, I was in my bed, with the light of the morning coming through the window and my lamp working perfectly once again.
	RAYNA: I wish I understood what happened there any better than you. It was like suddenly, the void turned against us.
	<< set $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Experience = true >>
	<< jump SecondLoopAaldenburg3 >>
-> "Where are you right now?" << if $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Identity == false >>
	SASHA: "Where are you right now?" 
    RAYNA: I work and live at the lighthouse, at the island on the void lake. I am sort of an on-site mechanic, I deal with electrical stuff. 
    RAYNA: So, I stay at the island for several days a week so I can always be ready to fix power outages and other problems.
    RAYNA: I was bummed out that I had to stay at the island alone all day while the parade was happening, but now it has gotten even worse.
    RAYNA: Now… I'm trapped here, waiting for the world to end again, completely alone and helpless… (voice starts warbling)
     << set $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Identity = true >>
     << jump SecondLoopAaldenburg3 >>
-> "Why do you call it a 'loop'? Can we really be sure this time travel is going to happen again?" << if $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Time == false 
	SASHA: "Why do you call it a 'loop'? Can we really be sure this time travel is going to happen again?"
    RAYNA: Well, I [i]don't know[/i], what would [i]you[/i] call this?
     << set $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Time = true >>
     << jump SecondLoopAaldenburg3 >>
-> "What should I do now?" << if $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Time == true && $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Identity == true && $AaldenburgLoop2Tree1Experience == true >>
	SASHA: "What should I do now?"
    RAYNA: I think we should meet, so we can make a real plan together.
	RAYNA: But.. I can’t leave the island at all. I don’t want to, because my post is too important to leave unguarded. A blackout can be so deadly in this town. But I also just couldn’t even if I wanted to.
	RAYNA: There is no boat on the island. I realise now how incredibly stupid of an oversight that sounds like, but normally there are Ferrymen constantly at the ready, and there is always at least one on the island at all times.
	RAYNA: It just happens that there is one day of the year where there are no ferries to and from the island. 
	RAYNA: Just… Figure out some way to get here. [i]Please.[/i]
	RAYNA: I’m sorry to be putting this responsibility on you, but I really need to meet you. I need to see another person agai--
	RADIO: (static)
	DR. AALDENBURG: Connection terminated. Total time: Five minutes and seven seconds.


-> "What happened? Where did she go?"
	SASHA: "What happened? Where did she go?"
    DR. AALDENBURG: The connection just cut. It is not that complicated.
    DR. AALDENBURG: Your radio is still functional, so she must have just stopped broadcasting. Finger misslip, it happens.
    -> "Do you have any idea of how I can get to the island? Can I swim there, or do I need a boat?"
